---
import { feeds } from "../build/feeds";
import { origin } from "../build/origin";
import Subscribe from "../components/Subscribe.astro";
import { about } from "../generated/about";
import { meta } from "../generated/meta";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { kindFeedPath, webcalUrl, ALL_EVENTS_FEED } from "../lib/feeds";
import { kindName } from "../lib/landing";
import ReportForm from "../components/ReportForm.astro";
import { siteConfig } from "../site.config";

const curationRules = `${siteConfig.repoUrl}/blob/main/prompts/curation-prompt.md`;
const extractionRules = `${siteConfig.repoUrl}/blob/main/prompts/extraction-rules.md`;
// Numbers in prose are words (house style); the staleness banner shows once the run is more than this many days old.
const WORDS = ["zero", "one", "two", "three", "four", "five", "six", "seven", "eight", "nine", "ten", "eleven", "twelve"];
const staleAfter = WORDS[siteConfig.staleAfterDays] ?? String(siteConfig.staleAfterDays);
const allEvents = feeds.allEvents.events.length;
---
<Base title="About" description="How this list is made, and how to report a wrong listing.">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About</h1>

    <h2>What this is</h2>
    <p>{siteConfig.tagline} It is for anyone in or near the city who wants to know what is on without wading through sponsored posts or a feed. It is free, and it has no ads and no accounts. It counts page views with Vercel Web Analytics, which sets no cookies, keeps nothing that identifies you, and loads from this site rather than anyone else's servers; nothing else is measured. Events you save stay in your own browser.</p>

    <h2>How it is made</h2>
    <p>Once a week a research job reads venue and organizer pages directly. A language model reads each page and writes the listing: its title, date, venue, and a short description; code then checks that the date and venue it quoted are on the page. A date and a venue are published only when they were read from the venue's or organizer's own page, never from a listings site or a search result; when the job cannot cite one, the event is held back rather than guessed at. The rules it reads pages by are <a href={extractionRules}>written down in the repository</a>.</p>
    <p>Every event shows when it was last verified, and the stamp under the site name says when the job last ran. <a href="/changes">What changed</a> lists what the last run added, moved, and took off. When the last run is more than {staleAfter} days old, every page says so.</p>

    <h2 id="for-venues">For venues</h2>
    <p>The research job fetches pages once a week as <code>{about.crawler}</code>, with the user agent <code>{about.userAgent}</code>. It obeys <code>robots.txt</code>: to keep it off your site, add a <code>User-agent: {about.crawler}</code> group with <code>Disallow: /</code>. Each listing links to your own event page.</p>
    <p>To correct a listing, use <a href="#wrong-listing">Corrections</a> below, or "Wrong? Report it" on the event's page; both are public. For anything else, such as asking for your calendar page to be read, <a href={`${siteConfig.repoUrl}/issues/new`}>open an issue on GitHub</a>.</p>

    <h2>What a pick is</h2>
    <p>A pick, tagged Don't miss, is a one-off or a limited run worth going out of your way for. It is an editorial call, made by a model under <a href={curationRules}>written rules</a> kept in the repository, and each one comes with a one-line reason. Recurring events are never picks; they are listed under Always there instead.</p>

    <h2 id="subscribe">In your calendar</h2>
    <Subscribe origin={origin} />
    <p>Or subscribe to <a href={webcalUrl(origin, ALL_EVENTS_FEED)}>everything listed</a> ({allEvents === 1 ? "1 event" : `${allEvents} events`}), or to one kind: {meta.kinds.map((kind, i) => <>{i > 0 && ", "}<a href={webcalUrl(origin, kindFeedPath(kind))}>{kindName(kind).toLowerCase()}</a></>)}. For Google Calendar or Outlook, each one's address is the same with <code>https://</code> in place of <code>webcal://</code>.</p>

    <h2>Keyboard shortcuts</h2>
    <p>In the <a href="/explore">explorer</a>, press <kbd>/</kbd> to jump to search, and <kbd>Esc</kbd> to clear it, then again to leave it.</p>
    {/* Shown by the script below, which is what reads and keeps the choice; without JavaScript there is no shortcut. */}
    <p class="shortcut-setting" hidden><label><input type="checkbox" id="shortcut-on" checked> Jump to search with <kbd>/</kbd></label> <span class="note">Kept in this browser.</span></p>

    <h2>The data</h2>
    <p>Every active event is at <a href="/events.json">/events.json</a>, with the fields this site shows. It is free to reuse under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>: credit KC This Week with a link, and say if you changed it. Prices and age limits are quoted from venues' pages and stay theirs. For language models and other readers, <a href="/llms.txt">/llms.txt</a> describes the site, the dataset, and how to cite it.</p>

    <h2 id="wrong-listing">Corrections</h2>
    <p>If a date, venue, or anything else is wrong, say so here; the report is posted publicly on GitHub. On an event's own page, "Wrong? Report it" sends the event's pages with it.</p>
    <ReportForm summary="Report a wrong listing" />

    <h2>The code</h2>
    <p>The research job and this site are open source, in <a href={siteConfig.repoUrl}>one repository on GitHub</a>.</p>

    <h2>Support</h2>
    <p>The site is free and has no ads. Each weekly run costs a few dollars in model and search fees; if the list is useful to you, a coffee helps cover them.</p>
    <p class="support-row"><a class="support" href={siteConfig.supportUrl}><img src="/buy-me-a-coffee.png" alt="Support KC This Week on Buy Me a Coffee" width="171" height="48"></a></p>
  </article>
</Base>

<script>
  // lib/shortcuts.ts's setting, written out rather than imported: an import would put the module in a chunk of its
  // own, one more request for the explorer (shortcuts.test.ts holds the key and values to the module's).
  const KEY = "kc-events.shortcuts";
  const box = document.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>("#shortcut-on")!;
  try {
    box.checked = localStorage.getItem(KEY) !== "off";
    box.addEventListener("change", () => {
      try { if (box.checked) localStorage.removeItem(KEY); else localStorage.setItem(KEY, "off"); } catch { /* blocked storage */ }
    });
    box.closest<HTMLElement>(".shortcut-setting")!.hidden = false;
  } catch {
    // blocked storage: the setting could not be kept, so it stays out of sight
  }
</script>

<style>
  h1 { margin: var(--space-8) 0 var(--space-4); }
  h2 { font-size: var(--text-lg); margin: var(--space-8) 0 var(--space-2); }
  p + p { margin-top: var(--space-3); }
  .support-row { text-align: center; }
  /* The checkbox and its words fill a 44 px target, the design's size for an action. */
  .shortcut-setting label { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: var(--space-2); min-height: var(--tap); cursor: pointer; }
  .shortcut-setting input { margin: 0; accent-color: var(--accent); }
  .shortcut-setting .note { color: var(--fg-muted); font-size: var(--text-sm); }
  .support { display: inline-block; border-radius: 10px; }
  .support img { display: block; }
</style>
